+Thirty-second request (2026-09-29), "place-line": a bare geometric-construction
+spec, no reference link — place a unit segment, then a second at a random angle
+touching an endpoint of the first *along its own length*, then require every
+further segment to touch the untouched endpoints of two other segments with no
+other intersections. The spec left the later segments' length completely open
+(only segment 1 was explicitly "unit length"). Consistent with the standing
+literal-math pattern, but the move here was picking the one length convention
+that makes the rule well-posed rather than leaving it a free parameter: made
+every segment unit length, since that is what turns "touch two free endpoints
+along its length" into a fully determined construction (and gives the process a
+non-arbitrary halting condition — two points over 1 unit apart in a line simply
+can't be joined). Flagged the convention explicitly on-page, not just in
+NOTE.txt/BRIEF — matches the domino-upright/Pentacell habit of putting a
+resolved ambiguity where it's checkable against what the requester had in mind,
+for a spec whose ambiguity was geometric rather than purely terminological.
+Also used randomized rejection sampling (up to 500 tries per new segment)
+rather than exhaustive search for finding a legal placement, and said plainly
+in BRIEF that a single "no room found" result isn't proof the configuration is
+actually full — worth watching whether a future follow-up pushes for the
+exhaustive/backtracking version once they've played with it, same shape as the
+quartic-solver and imaginary-Littlewood shortcuts being called out later.
